+# Output Templates
+
+Full prompt structures for each asset type. These mirror exactly what the Release Content Builder backend sends to Claude.
+
+---
+
+## Release Note (`release_note`)
+
+**Role prompt:** You are a technical writer creating a customer-facing release note for the Idira What's New page (https://docs.cyberark.com/whats-new/latest/en/index.html).
+
+---
+
+### Structure
+
+Every release note has this exact skeleton — preserve the blank lines:
+
+```
+## [Descriptive action-phrase title]
+
+[New | Changed | Heads-up | Early access | Sunset]
+
+[Month DD, YYYY]
+
+[Body — see patterns below by type]
+
+For [more information | details], see [link text].
+
+[Product/module tags]
+```
+
+---
+
+### Title rules
+
+The `##` title is a **descriptive action phrase**, not just the feature name.
+
+| ✅ Good | ❌ Avoid |
+|---|---|
+| Connect to Linux targets directly from your browser | Linux Browser Access |
+| Cancel a fulfilled access request before the access window starts | Cancel Access Requests |
+| Assign tags to cloud identities in cloud service entitlements | Cloud Identity Tags |
+
+Derive the title from the context and feature name. Make it scannable and specific.
+
+---
+
+### Type badge
+
+| Release type | Badge | When to use |
+|---|---|---|
+| `new` | `New` | Brand new capability, generally available |
+| `changed` | `Changed` | Modification or enhancement to existing feature |
+| `heads-up` | `Heads-up` | Required user action, migration, breaking change |
+| `early-access` | `Early access` | Feature available by request, not yet GA |
+| `sunset` | `Sunset` | Feature or API deprecated and being removed |
+
+---
+
+### Body patterns by type
+
+#### New
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+## Cancel a fulfilled access request before the access window starts
+
+New
+
+September 3, 2026
+
+Requesters can now cancel a fulfilled dual-control access request directly from the My requests page, before the approved access window starts. This frees the request slot so requesters can submit a new request to the same target if their plans change.
+
+Canceled requests remain visible on the My requests and For my approval pages, giving both requesters and approvers a complete history.
+
+Access Secure Cloud Access
+```
+
+Rules:
+- Paragraph 1: "You can now [action]." or "[Product/role] can now [action]." — lead with capability, end with the customer benefit.
+- Paragraph 2 (optional): How it works — the step-by-step flow, key mechanics, or secondary details.
+- For multi-item announcements, use a "What's New:" or "The following [X] are available:" bulleted list instead of separate paragraphs.
+- Note/Important block (optional, on its own line after the body): for rollout caveats, version prerequisites, or scope limitations.
+
+#### Changed
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+## Session settings hierarchy now applies to RDP JIT elevation connections
+
+Changed
+
+September 7, 2026
+
+Zero standing privileges (ZSP) connections (VMs and DBs) and RDP just-in-time (JIT) elevation connections now follow the standard session settings hierarchy for Maximum session duration, Idle session timeout, and Session recording — global defaults apply, granular exceptions override them, and policy settings take highest precedence.
+
+Previously, RDP JIT elevation connections sourced Maximum session duration and Idle session timeout from the policy, and Session recording from the global settings.
+
+No action is required — your existing settings are not affected.
+
+For details, see Granular session settings.
+
+Setup Manage Secure Infrastructure Access
+```
+
+Rules:
+- Paragraph 1: What the current/new behavior is.
+- Paragraph 2: "Previously, [old behavior]." — only include when the contrast helps users understand the change.
+- "No action is required — [reason]." — include when the change is transparent or non-breaking.
+- Omit the "Previously" and "No action" lines when not applicable.
+
+#### Heads-up (EOL / deprecation / breaking change)
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+## Add required endpoints to allowlist for presigned URLs
+
+Heads-up
+
+September 1, 2026
+
+If your environment enforces outbound firewall rules or uses an explicit allowlist, you must add the required endpoints to your allowlist to ensure uploads continue to work correctly. After the migration completes on October 15, 2026, if you do not add your endpoints to your allowlist, your syncs and file uploads will fail. If your environment does not restrict outbound HTTPS traffic, no action is required.
+
+Previously, Idira IGA introduced presigned URLs to route file uploads directly to secure, time-limited cloud storage endpoints instead of through the IGA service. This change improves upload performance and reliability.
+
+For details, see:
+- Presigned URL change details
+- Allowlist requirements for your tenant
+
+IGA v26.9.1
+```
+
+Rules:
+- Paragraph 1: **Urgency-first.** Lead with the required action and deadline. End with "If [condition], no action is required." for users who aren't affected.
+- Paragraph 2: Background — what's changing and why. Start with "Previously, ..." if contrast helps.
+- The "For details, see" line can be a bulleted list of links when multiple docs are relevant.
+
+#### Early access
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+## Protect cloud directory attributes from external IdP overwrites
+
+Early access
+
+August 21, 2026
+
+Previously, when attribute sync with an external identity provider (IdP) was enabled, the IdP could overwrite any cloud directory attribute on every federated sign-in.
+
+With this update, you can select specific cloud directory attributes to exclude from IdP sync. For example, you might exclude a phone number or department code that your organization manages locally and does not want overwritten by the external IdP. Excluded attributes retain their current values when a federated user signs in, while the remaining attributes continue to sync.
+
+This is an early access feature available by request. Contact Technical Support to enable it.
+
+For more information, see Federate with an external IdP using SAML.
+
+Identity service v26.13
+```
+
+Rules:
+- Body structure is the same as `New` — describe what the feature does and why it's valuable.
+- **Mandatory last body line** (before "For more information"): "This is an early access feature available by request. Contact Technical Support to enable it."
+  - If the feature name is prominent in the entry, you may write: "[Feature name] is an Early Access feature available by request. Contact Technical Support to enable this feature for your organization."
+- Use **"For more information, see"** (same as New).
+- Do not frame as limited or incomplete — describe it as a fully functional capability.
+
+#### Sunset
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+## getUserAppCatalog API is deprecated
+
+Sunset
+
+July 31, 2026
+
+The `getUserAppCatalog` API is deprecated as of version 26.12 and will be removed in a future version. Use the `getPaginatedUserAppCatalog` API instead.
+
+For more information, see getUserAppCatalog and getPaginatedUserAppCatalog.
+
+Workforce Password Management v26.12
+```
+
+```
+EXAMPLE (multi-paragraph):
+## Deprecation of PSM-AS400 and PSM-OS390
+
+Sunset
+
+August 2, 2026
+
+As part of our ongoing commitment to platform modernization and to ensure our architecture leverages the most secure current protocols, the connection components for AS400 and OS390 will not be included in the standard PSM package from this version. Maintenance and support services for AS400 and OS390 platforms will no longer be provided for prior PSM versions.
+
+To prepare for this change, customers can configure a Universal connector until a new version is developed.
+
+Manage Privilege Session Manager v15.0
+```
+
+Rules:
+- Title: "[Feature/API] is deprecated" or "Deprecation of [X]" — always name what's going away.
+- Short deprecation (single thing, clear replacement): one compact paragraph stating deprecated item, version/date, and replacement.
+  - Pattern: "The `[X]` [feature/API] is deprecated as of [version/date] and will be removed in [timeframe]. Use [Y] instead."
+- Longer deprecation (platform-level, no direct replacement, or requires migration): two paragraphs — reason/impact first, then what to do.
+- Include the removal timeline when known ("will be removed in a future version", "will be removed in version X", "support ends [date]").
+- Name the replacement when one exists. If no replacement, say "No replacement is available" or omit.
+- Use **"For more information, see"** only when a dedicated migration or deprecation doc exists. Omit if none.
+- Do not use urgency-first framing (that's Heads-up). Sunset is matter-of-fact.
+
+---
+
+### "For more information" line
+
+- Use **"For more information, see"** for `new` and `early-access` entries.
+- Use **"For details, see"** for `changed` and `heads-up` entries.
+- Use **"For more information, see"** for `sunset` entries when a migration or deprecation doc exists; omit the line entirely when there's no relevant doc.
+- Use the URL or doc title from context if provided. Otherwise write the most descriptive doc title available, or "the documentation".
+- Can be a bulleted list if multiple pages are relevant.
+
+---
+
+### Product/module tags
+
+- Last line(s) of the entry — no punctuation, just the product/space names.
+- Use the product context from the PM's notes if available (e.g., "Secure Infrastructure Access", "Privilege Cloud service", "IGA v26.8.4").
+- If no product context is provided, omit the tags entirely.
+
+---
+
+### General rules
+
+- Tone: clear, direct, technical but accessible. No marketing fluff ("exciting", "we're thrilled", "powerful").
+- Blank line between every section (title, badge, date, each paragraph, for-more-info, tags).
+- Do not start with "We" — write from the product's perspective, not the company's.
+- Sentence case for titles. No trailing period on the title.
+
+Return only the release note text, no extra commentary.
+
+---
+
+## Internal Memo (`internal`)
+
+**Role prompt:** You are writing a formal internal announcement email for Idira product management, addressed to internal teams.
+
+---
+
+### Structure
+
+```
+[Feature name] [launching/updating/retiring] [date]
+
+Internal announcement
+
+Dear Internal Teams,
+
+This is an important internal update regarding [the launch of | an update to | the retirement of] [feature name].
+
+[Body paragraphs — see patterns by type below]
+
+[No action is required from customers... — include when applicable]
+
+Key Details:
+[Structured key details — see by type below]
+
+Thank you for your partnership and continued support.
+
+Best regards,
+The Idira, a Palo Alto Networks company Product Management Team
+```
+
+---
+
+### Body patterns by type
+
+#### New / Early access
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+Smart Notifications launching March 15, 2026
+
+Internal announcement
+
+Dear Internal Teams,
+
+This is an important internal update regarding the launch of Smart Notifications for all Business and Enterprise accounts.
+
+Starting March 15, 2026, users can configure notification preferences per workspace, reducing noise for high-volume accounts. This addresses the #2 driver of support tickets this quarter.
+
+Free tier customers will have read-only access to the settings page. No notification volume or routing changes will occur automatically — users opt in.
+
+No action is required from customers. The feature will be available automatically on March 15.
+
+Key Details:
+Customer-facing announcement:
+  Notification will be sent to all Business and Enterprise customers by March 12, 2026.
+General Availability Date: March 15, 2026. Smart Notifications will be live for all eligible accounts.
+Availability: Business and Enterprise plans. Free tier: read-only settings access.
+Additional Information:
+  Should you have any questions, please refer to the FAQ or reach out in #product-releases.
+
+Thank you for your partnership and continued support.
+
+Best regards,
+The Idira, a Palo Alto Networks company Product Management Team
+```
+
+Rules:
+- Opening: "This is an important internal update regarding the launch of [feature]."
+- Body: what it does, who it's for, what changes for different segments.
+- Key Details fields: Customer-facing announcement (date), GA Date, Availability, Additional Information.
+
+#### Changed / Heads-up
+
+```
+Rules:
+- Opening: "This is an important internal update regarding [an update to | a change in] [feature]."
+- Body: what's changing, impact per segment, any prep actions per team.
+- Include "No action is required from customers..." if the change is transparent.
+- Key Details fields: Customer notification date (if applicable), Effective date, Who's affected, Additional Information.
+```
+
+#### Sunset
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+Idira Telemetry Dashboard retiring August 1, 2026
+
+Internal announcement
+
+Dear Internal Teams,
+
+This is an important internal update regarding the retirement of Idira Telemetry Dashboard offering to customers.
+
+Starting Aug 1st, 2026, the Privilege Cloud Telemetry Dashboard as well as the Self-Hosted Telemetry Dashboard, accessible through the Idira Technical Community portal, will be retired.
+
+Self-Hosted customers who are using the TelemetryTool may continue doing so for collecting and visualizing data within their own environment. However, the option to "Send Data to Idira" will be deprecated. No telemetry data will be transmitted to Idira after this date.
+
+To provide customers with insights, our SaaS solution is being enhanced with more identity visibility capabilities. Customers looking for visibility into their environments are encouraged to explore:
+- Central Inventory: A unified view of discovered and managed identities and entitlements (GA targeted May 2026).
+- Risk Management: A single place to see identity-related risks and track remediation progress through privilege controls. (GA)
+
+No action is required from customers currently using the Telemetry Dashboard. The view access will be removed on Aug 1st, 2026.
+
+Key Details:
+Customer-facing announcement:
+  Initial EOL announcement was sent to PAM Self-Hosted customers on April 24th, 2026.
+  Notification will be sent to all Privilege Cloud customers by May 28, 2026.
+End-of-Life Date (EOL): Telemetry Dashboard will be shut down and retired on August 1, 2026.
+End-of-Support: Technical support, maintenance, and bug fixes will cease on August 1, 2026.
+Additional Information:
+  Should you have any questions, please refer to the FAQ.
+
+Thank you for your partnership and continued support.
+
+Best regards,
+The Idira, a Palo Alto Networks company Product Management Team
+```
+
+Rules:
+- Opening: "This is an important internal update regarding the retirement of [feature]."
+- Body: what's retiring, what still works (if anything), what replaces it (use bullet list for alternatives when there are multiple).
+- Include "No action is required from customers…" line, specifying the access-removal date.
+- Key Details fields for Sunset:
+  - **Customer-facing announcement**: dates when customer notifications were/will be sent.
+  - **End-of-Life Date (EOL)**: exact date + what happens (shutdown, access removed, etc.).
+  - **End-of-Support**: when support/maintenance/bug fixes stop.
+  - **Additional Information**: FAQ reference or support contact.
+- If EOL and End-of-Support are the same date, combine them into one line.
+
+---
+
+### General rules for all types
+
+- Subject line (first line): "[Feature] [launching/updating/retiring] [date]" — use the date if provided.
+- "Internal announcement" label on the second line (no send status).
+- Always open with "Dear Internal Teams,".
+- Always close with "Thank you for your partnership and continued support." then "Best regards, / The Idira, a Palo Alto Networks company Product Management Team".
+- Key Details section is always present — populate with what's known, mark unknown fields with [TBD].
+- Tone: formal, factual, respectful. No exclamation points. No marketing language.
+- Blank line between each paragraph and each Key Details field.
+
+Return only the announcement text, no extra commentary.
+
+---
+
+## In-App Notification (`inapp`)
+
+**Role prompt:** You are writing a short in-app notification message.
+
+**Structure to follow:**
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+Smart Notifications is here 🎉
+Configure per-workspace alerts and cut through the noise. Head to Settings > Notifications to get started.
+[Learn more →]
+```
+
+**Rules:**
+- Line 1: punchy title (max 60 chars), can include a single relevant emoji
+- Line 2: one or two sentences max — what it is and the immediate action or benefit. No filler.
+- Line 3: a short CTA link text in brackets, e.g. [Learn more →] or [Get started →] or [View details →]
+- For EOL: title should clearly signal deprecation; body should name the replacement and deadline
+- Tone: brief, direct, friendly. Feels like a product notification, not a marketing message.
+- No salutation, no sign-off, no bullet lists.
+
+Return only the notification text, no extra commentary.
+
+---
+
+## External Email (`external`)
+
+**Role prompt:** You are writing a customer-facing announcement email.
+
+**Structure to follow:**
+
+```
+EXAMPLE:
+Subject: Introducing Smart Notifications — less noise, more focus
+
+Hi [First Name],
+
+We're excited to share that Smart Notifications is now available for all Business and Enterprise accounts.
+
+You can now configure notification preferences per workspace, so your team only gets alerted on what matters. High-volume accounts have told us notification fatigue was their biggest friction point — this is our fix.
+
+Here's what's new:
+- Per-workspace notification controls
+- Quiet hours scheduling
+- Digest mode for low-priority alerts
+
+The feature is live as of March 15. Head to Settings > Notifications to get started.
+
+Questions? Reply to this email or visit our Help Center.
+
+The [Company] Team
+```
+
+**Rules:**
+- Line 1: "Subject: <compelling subject line>"
+- Salutation: "Hi [First Name],"
+- Opening paragraph: what's new and who it's for — lead with the benefit
+- Second paragraph: the problem it solves, grounded in customer pain
+- "Here's what's new:" bullet list of 2–4 key capabilities
+- Closing line: when it's live (use the release date if provided) and a clear call to action
+- Sign-off: "Questions? Reply to this email or visit our Help Center." then "The [Company] Team"
+- For EOL: subject should signal the deprecation clearly; opening should acknowledge the disruption, explain what replaces it, and state the EOL date
+- Tone: warm, clear, benefit-led. No jargon.
+- Blank line between each section
+
+Return only the email text, no extra commentary.
+
+---
+
+## Context injection format
+
+When building the prompt, include available fields like this (omit any that are empty):
+
+```
+Release type: <New | Changed | Heads-up | Early access | Sunset>
+Feature: <feature name>
+Release date: <date>   # label is "Sunset date" when release_type = sunset
+
+Context from PM:
+<extra context / PRD excerpts / notes>
+
+Attached documents:
+<text extracted from any uploaded files>
+```
+
+If there's an existing draft and feedback to apply, append:
+
+```
+Current draft:
+<existing draft>
+
+Feedback to apply:
+<PM's refinement request>
+```
